WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.332
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.385 --> 00:00:10.676
JEN FITZPATRICK: Hi, everybody.

00:00:10.676 --> 00:00:12.960
It's great to be
here with you today.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.050
My name's Jen Fitzpatrick.

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:17.940
And I lead our GEO
Group here at Google.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.910
My team is obsessed
with understanding

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:24.036
everything there is to
know about the real world.

00:00:24.036 --> 00:00:26.160
And we want to bring that
insight and understanding

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.660
of the real world to
people around the globe,

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:34.730
helping them navigate, explore,
and decide with confidence.

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:38.515
We do this with three main
experiences-- Google Maps,

00:00:38.515 --> 00:00:40.640
which lets you leave home
knowing that you'll never

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.010
get lost, local search, the
ability to come to Google

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:46.820
and ask anything you
might need to know

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:49.730
about a local business, whether
it's one in your neighborhood,

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:53.300
or one far around the world;
and Google My Business, which

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.110
enables millions of businesses
to be found on Google

00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:59.500
and share what's most
unique about them.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.250
Over a billion people
around the world

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:03.980
now use Google Maps
and local search.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:06.080
But the impact
doesn't stop there.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.510
We also see incredible
opportunity for businesses

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:11.330
to benefit from this deep
understanding of location

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:12.502
in the real world.

00:01:12.502 --> 00:01:14.710
And I'm really excited about
partnering with everyone

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:17.180
here today to build
location-based products

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:22.896
and services that have a big
impact on your businesses, too.

00:01:22.896 --> 00:01:24.520
I wanted to start
today by jumping back

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.490
in time a little bit.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.460
It's not too long
ago that if you

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:30.580
wanted to go
someplace unfamiliar,

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:32.530
you had to pull
out a paper atlas

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:35.110
and plan your route in advance.

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:37.200
Any of you remember
having that experience?

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.640
Feels like a distant memory.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.240
But I'm pretty sure most of
us can think back to a time

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.950
when that was the default.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:46.320
So 11 years ago,
Google Maps set out

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.490
with the audacious goal
of mapping the world.

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:52.580
We wanted to digitize those
paper maps, and the paper

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.660
Yellow Pages, too.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.250
And we wanted to do it not
just for one neighborhood,

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.900
or even one country.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:02.780
We wanted to do it for the
entire world-- every street

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:07.580
and every surface, every
business, every detail-- all

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:10.110
of it.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:13.820
Since then, we've not only
brought maps online all

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:17.370
around the world, we've also
done things like help people

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.790
experience corners of the
planet that they might otherwise

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:23.540
never get to see, from the
tops of the highest mountains

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:25.970
to the depths of the oceans.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:29.880
And of course, along the way,
we brought all those experiences

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.390
onto mobile devices,
as well, giving users

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:34.440
everywhere a trusted
guide to the real world

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.370
that they can bring
with them anywhere

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:38.420
to help them get where
they're going fast,

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:40.700
and to find the best places
to visit along the way.

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:46.010
To build these sorts of
powerful user experiences,

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:49.010
we've had to also build out
an incredibly rich and complex

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:51.060
understanding of the real world.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:52.830
And it turns out that
the underlying data

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:55.630
that powers these consumer
mapping experiences

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:58.190
can also fuel some incredibly
powerful enterprise

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:00.930
use cases, as well.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.450
Today, we have over
three million developers,

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:06.514
and over 10,000 paying
customers using our Maps APIs

00:03:06.514 --> 00:03:08.180
to do everything from
visualizing points

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:11.970
on a map to routing and
planning for deliveries,

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:14.510
to doing things like
preprograming TV remotes based

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:16.640
on their location.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:19.890
And looking forward, we're
building on these capabilities

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.750
and bringing together all of our
both historical and real-time

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:25.340
knowledge about the
world, combining it

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:29.660
with things like machine
learning and our cloud compute

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:33.090
capabilities, and using
that to provide business

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:37.301
with even more insights and
predictive capabilities.

00:03:37.301 --> 00:03:39.800
So today, I want to share with
you three key ways that we're

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.470
seeing enterprises currently
take advantage of our Google

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:45.946
Maps data and services to
help grow their businesses,

00:03:45.946 --> 00:03:47.820
and also share some
examples of what we think

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:51.320
is possible together today.

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:53.220
We see clearly how
Google Maps can

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:57.040
help you build better
experiences for your customers,

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.360
how it can help you
optimize your business,

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:03.950
and how it can help enable
smart, strategic decisions.

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:06.170
So I talked earlier today
about how we're constantly

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:09.420
seeking ways to make our
consumers' experiences better

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:10.970
with Google Maps.

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:14.030
And we see that same appetite
from our enterprise partners--

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:16.310
finding ways to use the
power of Google Maps

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:20.170
to deliver better experiences
to their end users.

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:22.000
For example, we
see auto companies

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.180
like Audi, Tesla, and BMW
leveraging our local business

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:26.870
listings in their
in-car nav systems

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:28.880
to help their drivers
easily find and get

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:30.930
to millions of businesses
and points of interest

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:33.030
around the world.

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:35.310
And we've also
helped transform how

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:37.570
people are getting around
today by doing things

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:39.330
like powering the base
maps and navigation

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:44.030
experiences for companies
like Uber, Lyft, and others.

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:45.930
In retail, we're
helping companies

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:48.420
like Crate and Barrel,
Target, and Best Buy

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:51.480
connect online actions
to offline foot traffic,

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:53.110
and in doing, so
helping them to drive

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:56.120
more feet into their stores.

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:58.630
And we see savvy online
sellers also doing things

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:01.110
like taking advantage of our
autocomplete capabilities

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:02.780
to make it easier
for the users to type

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:05.180
an address into their
shopping cart, which

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:08.050
does things like lead directly
to fewer abandoned carts,

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:11.490
and increased conversion rates.

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:13.492
Let me talk about a
fun one for a minute.

00:05:13.492 --> 00:05:15.200
Any of you remember
going to Disney World

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:18.780
and getting a giant, complicated
paper map of the park?

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:19.520
And it's crowded.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:21.620
And you're trying to figure out,
how do I get to Space Mountain?

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:23.078
And on no, if I go
there right now,

00:05:23.078 --> 00:05:25.440
is the line going
to be super long?

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:27.300
Disney's a super fun
place, but it can also

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:30.230
be overwhelming and
incredibly frustrating.

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:32.540
And so Disney Parks
had this grand vision

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:35.700
of building an in-park
app experience that

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:38.570
would let you do everything
from understand what the wait

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:41.070
times were to make
reservations for the rides

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:43.420
you wanted to go to,
and help you easily get

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:45.830
around anywhere in the park.

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:47.980
Well, it turns out that
to do these things well,

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:51.980
one of the things you need
is an incredibly detailed--

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:56.120
highly detailed-- digital map
of every corner of Disney.

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:57.790
And so several years
back, we embarked

00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:01.440
on a long-term, multi-year
partnership with Disney

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.309
to create just that.

00:06:03.309 --> 00:06:04.850
And as we got into
it, along the way,

00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:06.650
we discovered that
there was a lot more

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:08.940
we could do beyond
that, too-- fun things

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:12.099
like enabling contextual,
location-based experiences.

00:06:12.099 --> 00:06:14.140
So if you've got a son or
a daughter who's really

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:17.570
into Goofy, you can get a
notification in real time

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:20.360
that tells you when Goofy
happens to be nearby.

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:21.980
We also did things
like incorporating

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:24.280
all the in-park transit
systems and lighting up

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:26.030
turn-by-turn directions.

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:29.150
So now, you can literally
find the fastest way

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:31.420
to get to Splash Mountain
right now, whether it's

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:33.680
walking or taking the monorail.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:36.250
This was a really fun project
for us-- a great partnership.

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:38.130
And we're super
pleased to have helped

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:41.820
make the Happiest Place on
Earth just a little bit happier

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:44.402
for all of Disney's
many visitors.

00:06:44.402 --> 00:06:46.110
Let's talk about
another fun one briefly.

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:49.180
You just heard about Pokemon
Go from Phil and crew.

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:51.720
And their story is
a phenomenal example

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:57.150
of what's possible today for
consumer experiences leveraging

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:58.020
Google Maps.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:59.640
Pokemon Go took
our mapping data,

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:01.560
and built an incredible
augmented reality game

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:02.770
on top of it.

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:05.526
And if their early
success is any indication,

00:07:05.526 --> 00:07:07.150
we fully expect that
we're going to see

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:10.600
a lot more fun and interesting
augmented reality experiences

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:13.140
coming in the near
future, both for consumers

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:15.920
and for enterprises.

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:17.260
Here's another one.

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:19.100
As the Internet of
Things scales up,

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:21.830
location services are
becoming even more essential

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:24.469
for creating smart experiences.

00:07:24.469 --> 00:07:26.010
I don't know about
you, but who would

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:27.230
have thought a few years
ago that everything

00:07:27.230 --> 00:07:29.730
from your thermostat to your
light bulb to your refrigerator

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:32.420
is going to be connected,
and be able to be controlled

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:33.840
by your smartphone?

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:35.804
But it's not so
crazy to think about.

00:07:35.804 --> 00:07:37.470
In fact, it's not so
crazy at this point

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:39.590
to think about a world in
the not-too-distant future

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:41.506
where literally every
device in your household

00:07:41.506 --> 00:07:43.880
is connected in
one way or another.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:47.000
And so imagine how cool would
be if your light switches

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:48.740
and your thermostats
know that you're

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:50.245
about to pull into
your driveway,

00:07:50.245 --> 00:07:52.120
and that it's you coming
home, not your kids.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:54.870
And so it can turn on your
lights and the air conditioning

00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:59.170
according to your preferences
right before you get home.

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:00.990
Or knowing your car's
about to pull out

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:02.925
of the driveway, your
alarm and your door lock

00:08:02.925 --> 00:08:04.702
can automatically
disarm so you can

00:08:04.702 --> 00:08:06.160
carry your groceries
into the house

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:08.400
without having to put them down.

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:11.040
In a connected world,
location awareness

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:13.530
can fuel all sorts
of new and fun ways

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:17.850
to interact with the things
in our physical surroundings.

00:08:17.850 --> 00:08:19.460
That gives you a
very brief taste

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:22.060
of some of the many ways that
we are seeing businesses build

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:24.910
really game-changing
experiences for their customers

00:08:24.910 --> 00:08:28.310
on top of our data and
insights about the world.

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:29.870
So the second big
area that we're

00:08:29.870 --> 00:08:32.210
seeing major innovation
from enterprises

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:34.780
is in optimizing
their core business.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:36.370
One of our core
strengths in Maps

00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:38.130
is increasingly becoming
our understanding

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:40.350
of real-time traffic data.

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:42.650
And many businesses,
especially those that

00:08:42.650 --> 00:08:45.040
are in the business of
moving things in the world,

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:48.310
tap into this rich data to help
streamline their operations,

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:50.470
and optimize their workforce.

00:08:50.470 --> 00:08:52.840
So from transport and
logistic companies

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:56.324
to things like emergency
relief and disaster response,

00:08:56.324 --> 00:08:57.990
we're seeing more and
more organizations

00:08:57.990 --> 00:09:00.520
putting Maps at the center
of their operations,

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:03.110
and helping them more
efficiently locate, manage,

00:09:03.110 --> 00:09:05.980
and monitor their resources.

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:09.900
So one example, Fleetminder
used the Google Maps APIs

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:12.770
to map their vehicle locations
so their dispatchers could

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:15.110
view where they were, and
send them real-time updates

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:16.950
as things were changing.

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:19.250
And what we saw from
them is that this

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:23.880
drove direct savings that went
right to their bottom line.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:26.720
So to give you an
example, their CIO

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:29.050
has told us that by using
Google's mapping solutions,

00:09:29.050 --> 00:09:32.070
it's help them cut their
inefficient driving in half,

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:35.020
and it's helped reduce
incorrect deliveries that they

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:36.010
were providing by 20%.

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:41.135
But now, build on
that a little further.

00:09:41.135 --> 00:09:42.730
And let's imagine
what's possible

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:45.080
when that rel-time traffic
understanding can get baked

00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:47.300
much more deeply
into your systems,

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:48.970
or as we're beginning
to be able to do,

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:51.029
also predict what traffic
or movement might look

00:09:51.029 --> 00:09:53.070
like under different
conditions in the real world

00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:55.040
in the future.

00:09:55.040 --> 00:09:58.340
Imagine being able to ask things
like, how many extra taxis

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:00.620
should I have at 5:00 PM
in New York on a Thursday

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:03.430
when the Yankees are
playing, or can I

00:10:03.430 --> 00:10:06.720
guarantee a 15-minute delivery
window to my customers

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:09.510
when there's a Beyonce concert
happening in LA on Thursday

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:11.310
night?

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:13.810
These are increasingly
the types of questions

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:16.770
that customers are asking
us to help them with.

00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:19.280
They want to optimize and
route their fleets better based

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:21.477
on changing conditions
in the real world.

00:10:21.477 --> 00:10:24.060
And we're really excited to be
giving more and more businesses

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:26.830
the insights that they
need to flexibly respond

00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:28.750
to real-time,
real-world conditions.

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:34.410
Finally, our deep
understanding of location,

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:36.950
combined with machine
learning and the cloud,

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:40.360
provides incredible potential
to help enterprises make

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:43.520
smart, strategic decisions.

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:46.250
An example-- there's a
German insurance company

00:10:46.250 --> 00:10:48.260
called WGV Insurance.

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:50.820
And they created a risk
map on top of our data

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:53.134
to understand both where
their policyholders are,

00:10:53.134 --> 00:10:55.300
and to help the model with
different risk management

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:56.860
scenarios.

00:10:56.860 --> 00:10:58.650
So in less than six
weeks, they were

00:10:58.650 --> 00:11:00.880
able to take their
policyholder information,

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:03.420
overlay it together with things
like flood areas, weather

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:05.520
reports, and other
types of geodata,

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:07.730
to help them make better
decisions about how

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:09.860
to financially model
their business.

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:12.480
And I think it's a testament to
the power of the sort of data

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:15.250
and tooling that they were
able to fundamentally shift

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:17.490
how they did risk
assessment modeling in such

00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:18.530
a short period of time.

00:11:21.870 --> 00:11:24.700
We also see a lot of
businesses asking questions

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:28.070
about the real world that either
they don't have answers to,

00:11:28.070 --> 00:11:30.260
or that they're
spending very, very

00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:32.700
heavily on physical
infrastructure to answer.

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:34.450
And this is a big area
where understanding

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:36.520
of how the world moves
can help influence

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:38.350
important commercial
decisions out there

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:40.390
in the physical world.

00:11:40.390 --> 00:11:42.182
To give you one
example, we're currently

00:11:42.182 --> 00:11:44.390
in conversations with a
major city that traditionally

00:11:44.390 --> 00:11:46.515
has spent hundreds of
millions of dollars on things

00:11:46.515 --> 00:11:48.120
like putting sensors
in their roads,

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:50.240
or sending surveys out
to homes to find out

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:54.010
things about traffic
and commuting patterns.

00:11:54.010 --> 00:11:56.460
And we're talking with them
now about how they can instead

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:58.980
use our real-time traffic
data, machine learning,

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:01.510
and predictive analytics
to help make urban planning

00:12:01.510 --> 00:12:05.810
decisions easier, faster, more
accurate, and very importantly,

00:12:05.810 --> 00:12:08.250
far, far less expensive.

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:12.230
And it's just one example of
how our rich data and analytics

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:14.891
capabilities can really drive
change in the way companies

00:12:14.891 --> 00:12:17.140
go about gathering information
about physical movement

00:12:17.140 --> 00:12:18.820
in the world.

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:20.820
Just two weeks ago, we
announced the acquisition

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:22.830
of Urban Engines, who
built a location analytics

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:27.410
business around use cases like
transit and transportation.

00:12:27.410 --> 00:12:30.375
We want to be able to help
answer questions like,

00:12:30.375 --> 00:12:33.632
where are the next areas of
urban sprawl going to be?

00:12:33.632 --> 00:12:35.340
And can we use that
information to decide

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:37.090
where to build the
next highway, or where

00:12:37.090 --> 00:12:39.530
I should put my
next retail store,

00:12:39.530 --> 00:12:42.810
or even how much retail staff
should I plan to have on hand

00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:46.120
next Friday night based on what
the predicted level of foot

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:49.030
traffic nearby is going to be?

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:51.510
We're really excited about
the new opportunities

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:53.400
that this deep location
intelligence can

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:56.500
fuel, building on top of
this rich comprehensive

00:12:56.500 --> 00:13:02.130
understanding of the physical
world and how it moves.

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:04.460
Over the last
decade, Google Maps

00:13:04.460 --> 00:13:06.810
has made some
incredible strides.

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:08.837
And yet, I really,
quite honestly,

00:13:08.837 --> 00:13:10.920
feel like we're just at
the beginning of something

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:13.500
new and even more exciting.

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:16.620
It's particularly exciting to
me that the types of problems

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:19.700
that businesses are looking
to us to help them solve

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:21.540
are exponentially more
challenging than they

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:23.480
were even a few years back.

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:25.260
A few years ago, most
of the conversations

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:26.290
we were having with
businesses were

00:13:26.290 --> 00:13:28.040
around things like
how could we help

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:30.257
them visualize the real world?

00:13:30.257 --> 00:13:32.090
How could we do things
like show some custom

00:13:32.090 --> 00:13:34.360
data on top of a map?

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:36.700
But today, enterprises
are increasingly

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:39.120
asking us about much more
sophisticated location

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:43.330
problems-- ones that involve
planning, spending, insights,

00:13:43.330 --> 00:13:46.572
movements, predictions.

00:13:46.572 --> 00:13:48.280
In essence, we're
helping businesses move

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:50.220
beyond just
visualizing the world,

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:53.874
and helping them make
major, real-world decisions.

00:13:53.874 --> 00:13:55.540
And at the center of
it all is location,

00:13:55.540 --> 00:13:59.350
and this understanding of
how the real world moves.

00:13:59.350 --> 00:14:02.070
So as you've heard today, the
combination of our raw location

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:05.060
understanding and movement
data from the billion users

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:08.200
of our consumer products,
combined with Google's machine

00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:10.020
learning and cloud
compute power,

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:12.660
can change the game
very, very quickly

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:16.310
for a lot of industries,
from retail to real estate,

00:14:16.310 --> 00:14:18.960
transportation to
logistics, and more.

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:24.080
So if you or your customers
operate in the physical world,

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:27.040
then location should
matter deeply to you.

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:30.770
And if it does, we
invite you to talk to us.

00:14:30.770 --> 00:14:32.410
What fuels our
imagination more than

00:14:32.410 --> 00:14:34.790
anything are
real-world problems.

00:14:34.790 --> 00:14:37.630
And we'd love to invite you
all into a conversation with us

00:14:37.630 --> 00:14:39.280
about what your
business needs are,

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:41.990
and the problems that you're
most looking to solve.

00:14:41.990 --> 00:14:44.670
We are just at the
beginning of helping

00:14:44.670 --> 00:14:47.330
businesses unlock the potential
from this understanding

00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:48.500
of location.

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:50.550
And we're very excited to
partner with all of you

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:52.460
to bring these
opportunities to life.

00:14:52.460 --> 00:14:53.590
Thank you.

00:14:53.590 --> 00:14:56.040
[APPLAUSE]

